# The which-key popup as Rust paints it: the leader on the top edge —
# the ACTIVE profile's, so the default (standard) profile reads `Ctrl+K`
# rather than a `<leader>` it has no key for — group labels with their
# `+`, the keys typed so far in the title of a sub-popup (which since
# 2026-09-14 carries that group's own row, `Ctrl+K f  +find (7)`), and
# `esc to cancel` at the bottom. The leader is bound on its own AND a
# prefix, so the popup opens on the `timeoutlen` pause, not the press
# (`tests/e2e/chord_ctrl_k_prefix.test`).
# (The standard profile's Ctrl+K PAUSE lists its own Ctrl+K chords (since
# 2026-09-23 — `standard_ctrl_k_pause_menu.test`); the leader tree is
# `whichkey.leader`, the palette's "Leader menu (which-key)".)
command whichkey.leader
expect screen contains "┌ Ctrl+K "
expect screen contains "f → +find"
expect screen contains "e → explorer"
expect screen contains "q → close buffer"
expect screen contains "w → write/save"
expect screen contains "P → +pr"
expect screen contains "esc to cancel"
type f
expect screen contains "┌ Ctrl+K f  +find (7) "
expect screen contains "f → files"
expect screen contains "g → grep"
key esc
expect screen lacks "esc to cancel"
expect screen lacks "f → +find"
